WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.529
Are minority rights in India are under threat?

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:11.860
In a country known for pluralism, India’s
secularists face their biggest challenge yet.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.710
Vigilante attacks on Muslims have shocked

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.710
many Indians.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:19.539
A firebrand Hindu cleric with a thuggish private
militia now runs India’s largest state.

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:23.969
On social media and TV, Hindu culture warriors
promote hardline identity politics.

00:00:23.969 --> 00:00:26.359
India’s ruling Bharatiya Janata Party bears

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:30.210
some of the blame for this new extremism,
but there’s also something deeper going

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.210
on.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.690
Simply but, the model of Indian secularism
has failed.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.800
Tiptoeing around religious sensitivities,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.750
India let different religions implement their
own personal laws rather than have one law

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:41.780
for all.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.230
This meant customs like polygamy or divorce
by decree are legal for Muslims, but not for

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.829
Christians or Hindus.

00:00:47.829 --> 00:00:52.110
Championing group rights over individual rights,
and tolerating regressive religious practices,

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.949
bred Hindu resentment.

00:00:53.949 --> 00:00:55.620
Indian secularists are right to worry about

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.249
a new wave of bigotry.

00:00:57.249 --> 00:01:00.870
But to fight it they need to look forward,
not backward.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.780
Rather than go back to a failed model that
prioritized special rights for some, they

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.460
should champion equal rights for all.

